## an10.58
an10.58:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: | T: “Bhikkhus, wanderers of other sects may ask you:
an10.58:1.2 | S: ‘Reverends, all things have what as their root? What produces them? What is their origin? What is their meeting place? What is their chief? What is their ruler? What is their overseer? What is their core? What is their objective? What is their culmination?’ How would you answer them?” | T: (1) ‘In what, friends, are all things rooted? (2) Through what do they come into being? (3) From what do they originate? (4) Upon what do they converge? (5) By what are they headed? (6) What exercises authority over them? (7) What is their supervisor? (8) What is their core? (9) In what do they culminate? (10) What is their consummation?’ If you are asked thus, how would you answer them?”
an10.58:1.3 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “Bhante, our teachings are rooted in the Blessed One, guided by the Blessed One, take recourse in the Blessed One. It would be good if the Blessed One would clear up the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from him, the bhikkhus will retain it in mind.”
an10.58:2.1 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then listen, bhikkhus, and attend closely. I will speak.”
an10.58:2.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, Bhante,” those bhikkhus replied.
an10.58:2.3 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an10.58:3.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: | T: “Bhikkhus, if wanderers of other sects should ask you:
an10.58:3.2 | S: ‘Reverends, all things have what as their root? What produces them? What is their origin? What is their meeting place? What is their chief? What is their ruler? What is their overseer? What is their core? What is their objective? What is their culmination?’ | T: ‘What, friends, are all things rooted in? … … What is their consummation?’
an10.58:3.3 | S: You should answer them: | T: you should answer them as follows.
an10.58:3.4 | S: ‘Reverends, all things are rooted in desire. They are produced by application of mind. Contact is their origin. Feeling is their meeting place. Immersion is their chief. Mindfulness is their ruler. Wisdom is their overseer. Freedom is their core. Their objective is freedom from death. And extinguishment is their culmination.’ | T: “‘Friends, (1) all things are rooted in desire. (2) They come into being through attention. (3) They originate from contact. (4) They converge upon feeling. (5) They are headed by concentration. (6) Mindfulness exercises authority over them. (7) Wisdom is their supervisor. (8) Liberation is their core. (9) They culminate in the deathless. (10) Their consummation is nibbāna.’
an10.58:3.5 | S: When questioned by wanderers of other religions, that’s how you should answer them.” | T: “If you are asked thus, bhikkhus, it is in such a way that you should answer those wanderers of other sects.”
an10.58:3.6 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.59
an10.59:1.1 | S: “So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, you should train yourselves thus:
an10.59:1.2 | S: ‘Our minds will be consolidated as they were when we went forth, and arisen bad unskillful qualities will not occupy our minds. | T: ‘Our minds will be strengthened in accordance with the spirit of our going forth, and arisen bad unwholesome qualities will not obsess our minds.
an10.59:1.3 | S: Our minds will be consolidated in the perceptions of impermanence, not-self, ugliness, and drawbacks. Knowing what is fair and unfair in the world, our minds will be consolidated in that perception. Knowing  existence and nonexistence in the world, our minds will be consolidated in that perception. Knowing the origin and disappearance of the world, our minds will be consolidated in that perception. Our minds will be consolidated in the perceptions of giving up, fading away, and cessation.’ | T: (1) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of impermanence. (2) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of non-self. (3) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of unattractiveness. (4) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of danger. (5) We will know the even and uneven ways of the world, and our minds will be strengthened in this perception. (6) We will know the coming into being and extermination of the world, and our minds will be strengthened in this perception. (7) We will know the origination and passing away of the world, and our minds will be strengthened in this perception. (8) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of abandoning. (9) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of dispassion. (10) Our minds will be strengthened in the perception of cessation.’
an10.59:1.4 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: It is in such a way that you should you train yourselves.
an10.59:2.1 | S: When your minds are consolidated in these ten perceptions, you can expect one of two results: | T: “When a bhikkhu’s mind has been strengthened in accordance with the spirit of his going forth, and arisen bad unwholesome qualities do not obsess his mind—when his mind has been strengthened in the perception of impermanence … when his mind has been strengthened in the perception of cessation—one of two fruits is to be expected for him:
an10.59:2.2 | S: enlightenment in this very life, or if there’s residue left behind, non-return.” | T: either final knowledge in this very life or, if there is a residue remaining, the state of non-returning.”
an10.59:2.3 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.60
an10.60:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park.
an10.60:1.2 | S: Now at that time Venerable Girimānanda was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Girimānanda was sick, afflicted, and gravely ill.
an10.60:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
an10.60:2.1 | S: “Sir, Venerable Girimānanda is sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: “Bhante, the Venerable Girimānanda is sick, afflicted, and gravely ill.
an10.60:2.2 | S: Sir, please go to Venerable Girimānanda out of sympathy.” | T: It would be good if the Blessed One would visit him out of compassion.”
an10.60:2.3 | S: “Ānanda, if you were to recite to the mendicant Girimānanda these ten perceptions, it’s possible that after hearing them his illness will die down on the spot. | T: “If, Ānanda, you visit the bhikkhu Girimānanda and speak to him about ten perceptions, it is possible that on hearing about them his affliction will immediately subside.
an10.60:3.1 | S: What ten? | T: What are the ten?
an10.60:3.2 | S: The perceptions of impermanence, not-self, ugliness, drawbacks, giving up, fading away, cessation, dissatisfaction with the whole world, impermanence of all conditions, and mindfulness of breathing. | T: “(1) The perception of impermanence, (2) the perception of non-self, (3) the perception of unattractiveness, (4) the perception of danger, (5) the perception of abandoning, (6) the perception of dispassion, (7) the perception of cessation, (8) the perception of non-delight in the entire world, (9) the perception of impermanence in all conditioned phenomena, and (10) mindfulness of breathing.
an10.60:4.1 | S: And what is the perception of impermanence? | T: (1) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of impermanence?
an10.60:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an10.60:4.3 | S: ‘Form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness are impermanent.’ | T: ‘Form is impermanent, feeling is impermanent, perception is impermanent, volitional activities are impermanent, consciousness is impermanent.’
an10.60:4.4 | S: And so they meditate observing impermanence in the five grasping aggregates. | T: Thus he dwells contemplating impermanence in these five aggregates subject to clinging.
an10.60:4.5 | S: This is called the perception of impermanence. | T: This is called the perception of impermanence.
an10.60:5.1 | S: And what is the perception of not-self? | T: (2) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of non-self?
an10.60:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an10.60:5.3 | S: ‘The eye and sights, ear and sounds, nose and smells, tongue and tastes, body and touches, and mind and ideas are not-self.’ | T: ‘The eye is non-self, forms are non-self; the ear is non-self, sounds are non-self; the nose is non-self, odors are non-self; the tongue is non-self, tastes are non-self; the body is non-self, tactile objects are non-self; the mind is non-self, mental phenomena are non-self.’
an10.60:5.4 | S: And so they meditate observing not-self in the six interior and exterior sense fields. | T: Thus he dwells contemplating non-self in these six internal and external sense bases.
an10.60:5.5 | S: This is called the perception of not-self. | T: This is called the perception of non-self.
an10.60:6.1 | S: And what is the perception of ugliness? | T: (3) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of unattractiveness?
an10.60:6.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant examines their own body up from the soles of the feet and down from the tips of the hairs, wrapped in skin and full of many kinds of filth. | T: Here, a bhikkhu reviews this very body upward from the soles of the feet and downward from the tips of the hairs, enclosed in skin, as full of many kinds of impurities:
an10.60:6.3 | S: ‘In this body there is head hair, body hair, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, diaphragm, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, undigested food, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, synovial fluid, urine.’ | T: ‘There are in this body hair of the head, hair of the body, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, pleura, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, stomach, excrement, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, fluid of the joints, urine.’
an10.60:6.4 | S: And so they meditate observing ugliness in this body. | T: Thus he dwells contemplating unattractiveness in this body.
an10.60:6.5 | S: This is called the perception of ugliness. | T: This is called the perception of unattractiveness.
an10.60:7.1 | S: And what is the perception of drawbacks? | T: (4) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of danger?
an10.60:7.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an10.60:7.3 | S: ‘This body has much suffering and many drawbacks. For this body is beset with many kinds of affliction, such as the following. | T: ‘This body is the source of much pain and danger; for all sorts of afflictions arise in this body, that is,
an10.60:7.4 | S: Diseases of the eye, inner ear, nose, tongue, body, head, outer ear, mouth, teeth, and lips. Cough, asthma, catarrh, inflammation, fever, stomach ache, fainting, dysentery, gastric pain, cholera, leprosy, boils, eczema, tuberculosis, epilepsy, herpes, itch, scabs, smallpox, scabies, hemorrhage, diabetes, piles, pimples, and ulcers. Afflictions stemming from disorders of bile, phlegm, wind, or their conjunction. Afflictions caused by change in weather, by not taking care of yourself, by exertion, or as the result of past deeds. Cold, heat, hunger, thirst, defecation, and urination.’ | T: eye-disease, disease of the inner ear, nose-disease, tongue-disease, body-disease, head-disease, disease of the external ear, mouth-disease, tooth-disease, cough, asthma, catarrh, pyrexia, fever, stomach ache, fainting, dysentery, gripes, cholera, leprosy, boils, eczema, tuberculosis, epilepsy, ringworm, itch, scab, chickenpox, scabies, hemorrhage, diabetes, hemorrhoids, cancer, fistula; illnesses originating from bile, phlegm, wind, or their combination; illnesses produced by change of climate; illnesses produced by careless behavior; illnesses produced by assault; or illnesses produced as the result of kamma; and cold, heat, hunger, thirst, defecation, and urination.’
an10.60:7.5 | S: And so they meditate observing drawbacks in this body. | T: Thus he dwells contemplating danger in this body.
an10.60:7.6 | S: This is called the perception of drawbacks. | T: This is called the perception of danger.
an10.60:8.1 | S: And what is the perception of giving up? | T: (5) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of abandoning?
an10.60:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t tolerate a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought that has arisen, and they don’t tolerate any bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen, but give them up, dispel them, eliminate them, and obliterate them. | T: Here, a bhikkhu does not tolerate an arisen sensual thought; he abandons it, dispels it, terminates it, and obliterates it. He does not tolerate an arisen thought of ill will … an arisen thought of harming … bad unwholesome states whenever they arise; he abandons them, dispels them, terminates them, and obliterates them.
an10.60:8.3 | S: This is called the perception of giving up. | T: This is called the perception of abandoning.
an10.60:9.1 | S: And what is the perception of fading away? | T: (6) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of dispassion?
an10.60:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an10.60:9.3 | S: ‘This is peaceful; this is sublime—that is, the stilling of all activities, the letting go of all attachments, the ending of craving, fading away, extinguishment.’ | T: ‘This is peaceful, this is sublime, that is, the stilling of all activities, the relinquishment of all acquisitions, the destruction of craving, dispassion, nibbāna.’
an10.60:9.4 | S: This is called the perception of fading away. | T: This is called the perception of dispassion.
an10.60:10.1 | S: And what is the perception of cessation? | T: (7) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of cessation?
an10.60:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: Here, having gone to the forest, to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, a bhikkhu reflects thus:
an10.60:10.3 | S: ‘This is peaceful; this is sublime—that is, the stilling of all activities, the letting go of all attachments, the ending of craving, cessation, extinguishment.’ | T: ‘This is peaceful, this is sublime, that is, the stilling of all activities, the relinquishment of all acquisitions, the destruction of craving, cessation, nibbāna.’
an10.60:10.4 | S: This is called the perception of cessation. | T: This is called the perception of cessation.
an10.60:11.1 | S: And what is the perception of dissatisfaction with the whole world? | T: (8) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of non-delight in the entire world?
an10.60:11.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant lives giving up and not grasping on to the attraction and grasping to the world, the mental fixation, insistence, and underlying tendencies. | T: Here, a bhikkhu refrains from any engagement and clinging, mental standpoints, adherences, and underlying tendencies in regard to the world, abandoning them without clinging to them.
an10.60:11.3 | S: This is called the perception of dissatisfaction with the whole world. | T: This is called the perception of non-delight in the entire world.
an10.60:12.1 | S: And what is the perception of the impermanence of all conditions? | T: (9) “And what, Ānanda, is the perception of impermanence in all conditioned phenomena?
an10.60:12.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is horrified, repelled, and disgusted with all conditions. | T: Here, a bhikkhu is repelled, humiliated, and disgusted by all conditioned phenomena.
an10.60:12.3 | S: This is called the perception of the impermanence of all conditions. | T: This is called the perception of impermanence in all conditioned phenomena.
an10.60:13.1 | S: And what is mindfulness of breathing? | T: (10) “And what, Ānanda, is mindfulness of breathing?
an10.60:13.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, sits down cross-legged, sets their body straight, and brings mindfulness to the present. | T: Here, a bhikkhu, having gone to the forest, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty hut, sits down. Having folded his legs crosswise, straightened his body, and established mindfulness in front of him,
an10.60:13.3 | S: Just mindful, they breathe in. Mindful, they breathe out. | T: just mindful he breathes in, mindful he breathes out.
an10.60:13.4 | S: Breathing in heavily they know: ‘I’m breathing in heavily.’ Breathing out heavily they know: ‘I’m breathing out heavily.’ | T: “Breathing in long, he knows: ‘I breathe in long’; or breathing out long, he knows: ‘I breathe out long.’
