## an10.61
an10.61:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, it is said that no prior point of ignorance is found, before which there was no ignorance, and afterwards it came to be. | T: “Bhikkhus, this is said: ‘A first point of ignorance, bhikkhus, is not seen such that before this there was no ignorance and afterward it came into being.’
an10.61:1.2 | S: And yet it is evident that there is a specific condition for ignorance. | T: Still, ignorance is seen to have a specific condition.
an10.61:2.1 | S: I say that ignorance is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: “I say, bhikkhus, that ignorance has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.2 | S: And what is the fuel for ignorance? | T: And what is the nutriment for ignorance?
an10.61:2.3 | S: You should say: ‘The five hindrances.’ | T: It should be said: the five hindrances.
an10.61:2.4 | S: I say that the five hindrances are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: The five hindrances, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.5 | S: And what is the fuel for the five hindrances? | T: And what is the nutriment for the five hindrances?
an10.61:2.6 | S: You should say: ‘The three kinds of misconduct.’ | T: It should be said: the three kinds of misconduct.
an10.61:2.7 | S: I say that the three kinds of misconduct are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: The three kinds of misconduct, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.8 | S: And what is the fuel for the three kinds of misconduct? | T: And what is the nutriment for the three kinds of misconduct?
an10.61:2.9 | S: You should say: ‘Lack of sense restraint.’ | T: It should be said: non-restraint of the sense faculties.
an10.61:2.10 | S: I say that lack of sense restraint is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Non-restraint of the sense faculties, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.11 | S: And what is the fuel for lack of sense restraint? | T: And what is the nutriment for non-restraint of the sense faculties?
an10.61:2.12 | S: You should say: ‘Lack of mindfulness and situational awareness.’ | T: It should be said: lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension.
an10.61:2.13 | S: I say that lack of mindfulness and situational awareness is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.14 | S: And what is the fuel for lack of mindfulness and situational awareness? | T: And what is the nutriment for lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension?
an10.61:2.15 | S: You should say: ‘Irrational application of mind.’ | T: It should be said: careless attention.
an10.61:2.16 | S: I say that irrational application of mind is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Careless attention, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.17 | S: And what is the fuel for irrational application of mind? | T: And what is the nutriment for careless attention?
an10.61:2.18 | S: You should say: ‘Lack of faith.’ | T: It should be said: lack of faith.
an10.61:2.19 | S: I say that lack of faith is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Lack of faith, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.20 | S: And what is the fuel for lack of faith? | T: And what is the nutriment for lack of faith?
an10.61:2.21 | S: You should say: ‘Listening to an untrue teaching.’ | T: It should be said: not hearing the good Dhamma.
an10.61:2.22 | S: I say that listening to an untrue teaching is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Not hearing the good Dhamma, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:2.23 | S: And what is the fuel for listening to an untrue teaching? | T: And what is the nutriment for not hearing the good Dhamma?
an10.61:2.24 | S: You should say: ‘Associating with untrue persons.’ | T: It should be said: not associating with good persons.
an10.61:3.1 | S: In this way, when the factor of associating with untrue persons is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of listening to an untrue teaching. When the factor of listening to an untrue teaching is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of lack of faith … irrational application of mind … lack of mindfulness and situational awareness … lack of sense restraint … the three kinds of misconduct … the five hindrances. When the five hindrances are fulfilled, they fulfill ignorance. | T: “Thus not associating with good persons, becoming full, fills up not hearing the good Dhamma. Not hearing the good Dhamma, becoming full, fills up lack of faith. Lack of faith, becoming full, fills up careless attention. Careless attention, becoming full, fills up lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension. Lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension, becoming full, fills up non-restraint of the sense faculties. Non-restraint of the sense faculties, becoming full, fills up the three kinds of misconduct. The three kinds of misconduct, becoming full, fill up the five hindrances. The five hindrances, becoming full, fill up ignorance.
an10.61:3.2 | S: That’s the fuel for ignorance, and that’s how it’s fulfilled. | T: Thus there is nutriment for ignorance, and in this way it becomes full.
an10.61:4.1 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily on a mountain top, and the water flows downhill to fill the hollows, crevices, and creeks. As they become full, they fill up the pools. The pools fill up the lakes, the lakes fill up the streams, and the streams fill up the rivers. And as the rivers become full, they fill up the ocean. | T: “Just as, when it is raining and the rain pours down in thick droplets on a mountaintop, the water flows down along the slope and fills the clefts, gullies, and creeks; these, becoming full, fill up the pools; these, becoming full, fill up the lakes; these, becoming full, fill up the streams; these, becoming full, fill up the rivers; and these, becoming full, fill up the great ocean;
an10.61:4.2 | S: That’s the fuel for the ocean, and that’s how it’s filled up. | T: thus there is nutriment for the great ocean, and in this way it becomes full.
an10.61:5.1 | S: In the same way, when the factor of associating with untrue persons is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of listening to an untrue teaching. When the factor of listening to an untrue teaching is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of lack of faith … irrational application of mind … lack of mindfulness and situational awareness … lack of sense restraint … the three kinds of misconduct … the five hindrances. When the five hindrances are fulfilled, they fulfill ignorance. | T: So too, not associating with good persons, becoming full, fills up not hearing the good Dhamma…. The five hindrances, becoming full, fill up ignorance.
an10.61:5.2 | S: That’s the fuel for ignorance, and that’s how it’s fulfilled. | T: Thus there is nutriment for ignorance, and in this way it becomes full.
an10.61:6.1 | S: I say that knowledge and freedom are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: “I say, bhikkhus, that (1) true knowledge and liberation have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.2 | S: And what is the fuel for knowledge and freedom? | T: And what is the nutriment for true knowledge and liberation?
an10.61:6.3 | S: You should say: ‘The seven awakening factors.’ | T: It should be said: (2) the seven factors of enlightenment.
an10.61:6.4 | S: I say that the seven awakening factors are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: The seven factors of enlightenment, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.5 | S: And what is the fuel for the seven awakening factors? | T: And what is the nutriment for the seven factors of enlightenment?
an10.61:6.6 | S: You should say: ‘The four kinds of mindfulness meditation.’ | T: It should be said: (3) the four establishments of mindfulness.
an10.61:6.7 | S: I say that the four kinds of mindfulness meditation are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: The four establishments of mindfulness, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.8 | S: And what is the fuel for the four kinds of mindfulness meditation? | T: And what is the nutriment for the four establishments of mindfulness?
an10.61:6.9 | S: You should say: ‘The three kinds of good conduct.’ | T: It should be said: (4) the three kinds of good conduct.
an10.61:6.10 | S: I say that the three kinds of good conduct are fueled by something, they’re not unfueled. | T: The three kinds of good conduct, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.11 | S: And what is the fuel for the three kinds of good conduct? | T: And what is the nutriment for the three kinds of good conduct?
an10.61:6.12 | S: You should say: ‘Sense restraint.’ | T: It should be said: (5) restraint of the sense faculties.
an10.61:6.13 | S: I say that sense restraint is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Restraint of the sense faculties, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.14 | S: And what is the fuel for sense restraint? | T: And what is the nutriment for restraint of the sense faculties?
an10.61:6.15 | S: You should say: ‘Mindfulness and situational awareness.’ | T: It should be said: (6) mindfulness and clear comprehension.
an10.61:6.16 | S: I say that mindfulness and situational awareness is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Mindfulness and clear comprehension, too, I say, have a nutriment; they are not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.17 | S: And what is the fuel for mindfulness and situational awareness? | T: And what is the nutriment for mindfulness and clear comprehension?
an10.61:6.18 | S: You should say: ‘Rational application of mind.’ | T: It should be said: (7) careful attention.
an10.61:6.19 | S: I say that rational application of mind is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Careful attention, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.20 | S: And what is the fuel for rational application of mind? | T: And what is the nutriment for careful attention?
an10.61:6.21 | S: You should say: ‘Faith.’ | T: It should be said: (8) faith.
an10.61:6.22 | S: I say that faith is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Faith, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.23 | S: And what is the fuel for faith? | T: And what is the nutriment for faith?
an10.61:6.24 | S: You should say: ‘Listening to the true teaching.’ | T: It should be said: (9) hearing the good Dhamma.
an10.61:6.25 | S: I say that listening to the true teaching is fueled by something, it’s not unfueled. | T: Hearing the good Dhamma, too, I say, has a nutriment; it is not without nutriment.
an10.61:6.26 | S: And what is the fuel for listening to the true teaching? | T: And what is the nutriment for hearing the good Dhamma?
an10.61:6.27 | S: You should say: ‘Associating with true persons.’ | T: It should be said: (10) associating with good persons.
an10.61:7.1 | S: In this way, when the factor of associating with true persons is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of listening to the true teaching. When the factor of listening to the true teaching is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of faith … rational application of mind … mindfulness and situational awareness … sense restraint … the three kinds of good conduct … the four kinds of mindfulness meditation … the seven awakening factors. When the seven awakening factors are fulfilled, they fulfill knowledge and freedom. | T: “Thus associating with good persons, becoming full, fills up hearing the good Dhamma. Hearing the good Dhamma, becoming full, fills up faith. Faith, becoming full, fills up careful attention. Careful attention, becoming full, fills up mindfulness and clear comprehension. Mindfulness and clear comprehension, becoming full, fill up restraint of the sense faculties. Restraint of the sense faculties, becoming full, fills up the three kinds of good conduct. The three kinds of good conduct, becoming full, fill up the four establishments of mindfulness. The four establishments of mindfulness, becoming full, fill up the seven factors of enlightenment. The seven factors of enlightenment, becoming full, fill up true knowledge and liberation.
an10.61:7.2 | S: That’s the fuel for knowledge and freedom, and that’s how it’s fulfilled. | T: Thus there is nutriment for true knowledge and liberation, and in this way they become full.
an10.61:8.1 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily on a mountain top, and the water flows downhill to fill the hollows, crevices, and creeks. As they become full, they fill up the pools. The pools fill up the lakes, the lakes fill up the streams, and the streams fill up the rivers. And as the rivers become full, they fill up the ocean. | T: “Just as, when it is raining and the rain pours down in thick droplets on a mountaintop, the water flows down along the slope and fills the clefts, gullies, and creeks; these, becoming full, fill up the pools; these, becoming full, fill up the lakes; these, becoming full, fill up the streams; these, becoming full, fill up the rivers; and these, becoming full, fill up the great ocean;
an10.61:8.2 | S: That’s the fuel for the ocean, and that’s how it’s filled up. | T: thus there is nutriment for the great ocean, and in this way it becomes full.

