## an10.6
an10.6:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
an10.6:1.2 | S: “Could it be, sir, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this? They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “Bhante, could a bhikkhu obtain such a state of concentration that (1) he would not be percipient of earth in relation to earth; (2) of water in relation to water; (3) of fire in relation to fire; (4) of air in relation to air; (5) of the base of the infinity of space in relation to the base of the infinity of space; (6) of the base of the infinity of consciousness in relation to the base of the infinity of consciousness; (7) of the base of nothingness in relation to the base of nothingness; (8) of the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception in relation to the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception; (9) of this world in relation to this world; (10) of the other world in relation to the other world, but he would still be percipient?”
an10.6:1.3 | S: “It could be, Ānanda, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this. They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “He could, Ānanda.”
an10.6:2.1 | S: “But how could this be, sir?” | T: “But how, Bhante, could he obtain such a state of concentration?”
an10.6:3.1 | S: “Ānanda, it’s when a mendicant perceives: | T: “Here, Ānanda, a bhikkhu is percipient thus:
an10.6:3.2 | S: ‘This is peaceful; this is sublime—that is, the stilling of all activities, the letting go of all attachments, the ending of craving, fading away, cessation, extinguishment.’ | T: ‘This is peaceful, this is sublime, that is, the stilling of all activities, the relinquishing of all acquisitions, the destruction of craving, dispassion, cessation, nibbāna.’
an10.6:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this. They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: It is in this way, Ānanda, that a bhikkhu could obtain such a state of concentration that he would not be percipient of earth in relation to earth; of water in relation to water; of fire in relation to fire; of air in relation to air; of the base of the infinity of space in relation to the base of the infinity of space; of the base of the infinity of consciousness in relation to the base of the infinity of consciousness; of the base of nothingness in relation to the base of nothingness; of the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception in relation to the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception; of this world in relation to this world; of the other world in relation to the other world, but he would still be percipient.”
an10.6:3.4 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.7
an10.7:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda approached the Venerable Sāriputta and exchanged greetings with him.
an10.7:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Sāriputta: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to the Venerable Sāriputta:
an10.7:2.1 | S: “Could it be, reverend Sāriputta, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this? They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, could a bhikkhu obtain such a state of concentration that (1) he would not be percipient of earth in relation to earth; (2) of water in relation to water; (3) of fire in relation to fire; (4) of air in relation to air; (5) of the base of the infinity of space in relation to the base of the infinity of space; (6) of the base of the infinity of consciousness in relation to the base of the infinity of consciousness; (7) of the base of nothingness in relation to the base of nothingness; (8) of the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception in relation to the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception; (9) of this world in relation to this world; (10) of the other world in relation to the other world, but he would still be percipient?”
an10.7:3.1 | S: “It could be, Reverend Ānanda.” | T: “He could, friend Ānanda.”
an10.7:4.1 | S: “But how could this be?” | T: “But how, friend Sāriputta, could he obtain such a state of concentration?”
an10.7:4.2 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, this one time I was staying right here at Sāvatthī in the Dark Forest. | T: “On one occasion, friend Ānanda, I was dwelling right here in Sāvatthī in the Blind Men’s Grove.
an10.7:4.3 | S: There I gained a state of immersion like this. I didn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And I didn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And I didn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet I still perceived.” | T: There I attained such a state of concentration that I was not percipient of earth in relation to earth; of water in relation to water; of fire in relation to fire; of air in relation to air; of the base of the infinity of space in relation to the base of the infinity of space; of the base of the infinity of consciousness in relation to the base of the infinity of consciousness; of the base of nothingness in relation to the base of nothingness; of the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception in relation to the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception; of this world in relation to this world; of the other world in relation to the other world, but I was still percipient.”
an10.7:5.1 | S: “But at that time what did Reverend Sāriputta perceive?” | T: “But of what was the Venerable Sāriputta percipient on that occasion?”
an10.7:5.2 | S: “One perception arose in me and another perception ceased: ‘The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment. The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.’ | T: “One perception arose and another perception ceased in me: ‘The cessation of existence is nibbāna; the cessation of existence is nibbāna.’
an10.7:5.3 | S: Suppose there was a burning pile of twigs. One flame would arise and another would cease. | T: Just as, when a fire of twigs is burning, one flame arises and another flame ceases,
an10.7:5.4 | S: In the same way, one perception arose in me and another perception ceased: ‘The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment. The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.’ | T: so one perception arose and another perception ceased in me: ‘The cessation of existence is nibbāna; the cessation of existence is nibbāna.’
an10.7:5.5 | S: At that time I perceived that the cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.” | T: On that occasion, friend, I was percipient: ‘The cessation of existence is nibbāna.’”
an10.7:5.6 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.8
an10.8:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant is faithful but not ethical. | T: (1) “Bhikkhus, a bhikkhu may be endowed with faith but he is not virtuous;
an10.8:1.2 | S: So they’re incomplete in that respect, | T: thus he is incomplete with respect to that factor.
an10.8:1.3 | S: and should fulfill it, thinking: | T: He should fulfill that factor, thinking:
an10.8:1.4 | S: ‘How can I become faithful and ethical?’ | T: ‘How can I be endowed with faith and also be virtuous?’
an10.8:1.5 | S: When the mendicant is faithful and ethical, they’re complete in that respect. | T: But when a bhikkhu is endowed with faith and is also virtuous, then he is complete with respect to that factor.
an10.8:2.1 | S: A mendicant is faithful and ethical, but not learned. … | T: (2) “A bhikkhu may be endowed with faith and virtuous, but he is not learned …
an10.8:2.2 | S: they’re not a Dhamma speaker … | T: (3) … learned, but not a speaker on the Dhamma …
an10.8:2.3 | S: they don’t frequent assemblies … | T: (4) … a speaker on the Dhamma, but not one who frequents assemblies …
an10.8:2.4 | S: they don’t teach Dhamma to the assembly with assurance … | T: (5) … one who frequents assemblies, but not one who confidently teaches the Dhamma to an assembly …
an10.8:2.5 | S: they’re not an expert in the monastic law … | T: (6) … one who confidently teaches the Dhamma to an assembly, but not an expert on the discipline …
an10.8:2.6 | S: they don’t stay in the wilderness, in remote lodgings … | T: (7) … an expert on the discipline, but not a forest-dweller who resorts to remote lodgings …
an10.8:2.7 | S: they don’t get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when they want, without trouble or difficulty … | T: (8) … a forest-dweller who resorts to remote lodgings, but not one who gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life …
an10.8:2.8 | S: they don’t realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom, and live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: (9) … one who gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life, but not one who, with the destruction of the taints, has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, dwells in it.
an10.8:2.9 | S: So they’re incomplete in that respect, | T: “Thus he is incomplete with respect to that factor.
an10.8:2.10 | S: and should fulfill it, thinking: | T: He should fulfill that factor, thinking:
an10.8:2.11 | S: ‘How can I become faithful, ethical, and learned, a Dhamma speaker, one who frequents assemblies, one who teaches Dhamma to the assembly with assurance, an expert in the training, one who lives in the wilderness, in remote lodgings, one who gets the four absorptions when they want, and one who lives having realized the ending of defilements?’ | T: ‘How can I be endowed with faith … … and also be one who, with the destruction of the taints, has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, dwells in it?’
an10.8:3.1 | S: When they’re faithful, ethical, and learned, a Dhamma speaker, one who frequents assemblies, one who teaches Dhamma to the assembly with assurance, an expert in the training, one who lives in the wilderness, in remote lodgings, one who gets the four absorptions when they want, and one who lives having realized the ending of defilements, | T: (10) “But when a bhikkhu is i endowed with faith, ii virtuous, and iii learned; iv a speaker on the Dhamma; v one who frequents assemblies; vi one who confidently teaches the Dhamma to an assembly; vii an expert on the discipline; viii a forest-dweller who resorts to remote lodgings; ix one who gains at will, without trouble or difficulty, the four jhānas that constitute the higher mind and are pleasant dwellings in this very life; and x one who, with the destruction of the taints, has realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, dwells in it,
an10.8:3.2 | S: they’re complete in that respect. | T: then he is complete with respect to that factor.
an10.8:3.3 | S: A mendicant who possesses these ten qualities is impressive all around, and is complete in every respect.” | T: “A bhikkhu who possesses these ten qualities is one who inspires confidence in all respects and who is complete in all aspects.”
an10.8:3.4 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


